(December 20, 1993)	Howdy!	Here's my humble attempt at porting Graham Nelson's nifty Z-code compilerto the OS-9/680x0 (OSK for short) operating system.	Mr. Nelson, I salute you. Your documentation is superb; it made theprogram a breeze to port. 8-)	Contained in this archive is all the original stuff from the generic UNIXdistribution, with one additional thing: the file "informosk.c" is a K&R-izedversion of the C source for the compiler. Since I don't have Microware'srelatively new Ultra-C (which is supposed to be a full ANSI compiler), I hadto change all the function headers back to the old K&R syntax, get rid of some'const' keywords, etc. As a result, you should be able to compile informosk.cwith the regular Microware C compiler. If you have GNU C, it should be prettyeasy to get the ANSI version ("inform.c") to work.	To compile informosk.c, use the following commandline (the -K0WCL flagsforce longword code references, otherwise r68 complains about out of rangebranches):$ cc -K0WCL -E=1 -M=16k informosk.c -F=inform	The compiler generates the dejavu.z3 demo properly, as far as I know, so itshould be good. I compiled it on an MM/1 computer with OS9 v2.4.	-Russ Magee